index 544d3dce10..11ac07e14e 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -616,7 +616,7 @@ For Claude-managed bead workers, the canonical `worker` profile is reified throu
 
 Landing convergence is infrastructure-owned, not author-agent busywork. After a lifecycle call returns an accepted detached queue submission or a published/queued PR with queue ownership, the author leaves the bead open/in-flight and moves to its next permitted slice (or idles for a one-shot worker). It must not run PR-check/status watches, update-branch loops, or repeated `caco agent land-pr` nudges. `caco agent land-pr` itself is status-only by default (one `gh pr view`, advisory, return); only explicit `--critical` or `CACO_LAND_PR_CLIENT_WATCH=1` restores its bounded client watch/nudge path, and `CACO_LAND_QUEUE_ENABLED` still makes it defer unconditionally. Queue/daemon controllers own convergence, terminal-failure routing, and confirmed-merge closeout; force/bypass remains an explicit audited operator/controller path for changes classified critical.
 
-For normal Cacophony typed-Aviator submissions, hosted GitHub required CI owns final ready-time test and lint validation. Authors may run one very small exact smoke while developing when it answers a concrete uncertainty, but must not run a final local or daemon-queued Cargo/check/clippy/build/docs gate that CI will immediately repeat on the same self-hosted capacity. Ready work is committed, submitted, and left to the queue. Broader checks require explicit missing hosted coverage, test-infrastructure, safety reproduction, device/platform acceptance, CI-failure diagnosis, or operator direction; projects without equivalent hosted enforcement retain their configured validation policy.
+For normal Cacophony typed-Aviator submissions, hosted GitHub required CI owns final ready-time test and lint validation. Authors may run one very small exact smoke while developing when it answers a concrete uncertainty, but must not run a final local or daemon-queued Cargo/check/clippy/build/docs gate that CI will immediately repeat on the same self-hosted capacity. Ready work is committed, submitted, and left to the queue. Broader checks require explicit missing hosted coverage, test-infrastructure, safety reproduction, device/platform acceptance, CI-failure diagnosis, or operator direction; projects without equivalent hosted enforcement retain their configured validation policy. Managed crash/restart resume nudges preserve the foreground-only safety rule while restating these conditional lanes; they do not revive blanket `cargo test`/`cargo build`/`cargo clippy` preflight examples.
 
 Until provider-neutral retry automation lands, an Aviator Returned/Rejected/Conflict PR needs one owner-only manual repair step. After the original owner fixes/rebases and publishes the corrected branch, that owner runs `gh pr edit <PR> --remove-label blocked --add-label mergequeue`, keeps the bead claim and PR link, and moves on. Never clear/requeue another owner's PR, clear `blocked` before publishing a correction, or arm Track A/native auto alongside Aviator.
 
diff --git a/SPEC.md b/SPEC.md
index ade0d6d251..4c0bf34e18 100644
--- a/SPEC.md
+++ b/SPEC.md
@@ -5191,7 +5191,7 @@ Resolution rules:
 - `caco agent land-pr` is read-only/status-only by default: after preserving the authoritative `CACO_LAND_QUEUE_ENABLED` defer, it resolves the PR, performs exactly one `gh pr view`, reports state plus a no-babysit advisory, and returns without sleeping, updating the branch, or falling back. The legacy bounded client-side watch/nudge/fallback bridge runs only with explicit `--critical` or truthy `CACO_LAND_PR_CLIENT_WATCH`, for a genuinely critical land classified by an operator/controller (bd-c339e1).
 - force/bypass landing is not a routine authoring-agent fallback; it is reserved for an operator/controller-classified critical change through an explicit audited first-party route
 - a project may select a typed hosted merge queue for PR-backed direct intent; hosted submission is a handoff, so success means the PR is durably enqueued and explicitly reported as pending rather than falsely reported as landed
-- when a typed hosted queue has authoritative required CI, normal ready-time validation belongs to that hosted lane: authoring agents must not run a final local or daemon-queued test/check/clippy/build/docs gate that CI will immediately repeat. During implementation they may run only the smallest exact smoke needed for a concrete uncertainty; broader local validation requires explicit missing hosted coverage, test-infrastructure, safety reproduction, device/platform acceptance, CI-failure diagnosis, or operator direction. Projects without equivalent hosted enforcement retain their configured validation policy
+- when a typed hosted queue has authoritative required CI, normal ready-time validation belongs to that hosted lane: authoring agents must not run a final local or daemon-queued test/check/clippy/build/docs gate that CI will immediately repeat. During implementation they may run only the smallest exact smoke needed for a concrete uncertainty; broader local validation requires explicit missing hosted coverage, test-infrastructure, safety reproduction, device/platform acceptance, CI-failure diagnosis, or operator direction. Projects without equivalent hosted enforcement retain their configured validation policy. Crash/restart resume nudges must repeat this conditional lane contract while preserving foreground-only execution safety; they must not revive unconditional broad Cargo command examples (bd-7624ac)
 - CI path classification must consume the exact PR/stack delta selected by the caller, remain token-free/fork-safe, and fail to full lanes on empty, malformed, unreadable, unknown, workflow, Rust, or platform paths. The reusable classifier foundation emits stable `config-profile-only` vs `full` JSON/GitHub outputs for conservative checked-in `.cacophony` YAML/profile/instruction sources and explicit generated profile/plugin/docs artifacts; workflow wiring must preserve required check contexts and may not infer a broader ancestor-stack diff (bd-57ade7)
 - hosted-queue policy must be default-off, reuse the project's configured `gh` wrapper, preserve the agent-specific PR branch and `pr-in-flight` bead marker, fail closed when enqueueing fails, and suppress native auto-merge plus the daemon Track-A land queue for that PR so two landers cannot race
 - when the effective project enables typed hosted_queue, a nonpersistent worker must not local-direct land or silently fall back to `pr_review`: it calls reintegrate with no mode/backend override, effective direct intent routes through the pull-request backend, Caco ensures the hosted trigger and records queue ownership, and explicit `pr_auto_merge`/native auto is refused. The worker/bead/PR remain owned while unmerged. After confirmed merge, the worker inspects checkout state and may complete idempotently or discard only when merged source is durable and the checkout is clean; dirty/untracked work is preserved and blocks silent teardown. Projects without hosted_queue retain their configured direct/review/artifact lifecycle, while persistent controllers/observers and release/tag actors retain explicit audited modes
